Hello Unmarked group

We are using N-mixture models for a slightly different purpose than usual. We want to estimate a given species predicted number of individuals in different eras based on findings from archaeological sites. Our "site" is an era and "occasions" are spatial replicates-  sampled sites.

We were able to predict the number of individuals in each era and predict detection in each sampled site, but we wanted to know if there's a way to predict the number of individuals in a specific sampling occasion (in our case site).

Thank you,

Hila Shamoon

From u4a635fe980c8c1cd at groups.google.com  Sun Nov 27 20:57:44 2016
From: u4a635fe980c8c1cd at groups.google.com (John Clare)
Date: Sun, 27 Nov 2016 20:57:44 +0000
Subject: Re: Predict number of individuals per occasion
Message-ID: <AERCeJLOXdU.DMV8yq3ZCQAJ@unmarked.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <AERCeJLOXdU.ji9JUt3QCQAJ@unmarked.groups.google.com>
References: <AERCeJLOXdU.ji9JUt3QCQAJ@unmarked.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/unmarked/c/AERCeJLOXdU/m/DMV8yq3ZCQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

 As traditionally performed, the N-mixture assumes that N is constant at a specific site for all occasions.  But there are some alternative formulations for single-visit N mixtures (Subhash Lele and Peter Solymos, I think) or dynamic N-mixture models that allow inference about N to made with a single visit (Dail and Madsen 2011, see pcountOpen in unmarked); another alternative might be to use something like a time-of-removal sort of approach (multinomial N-mixture, see gmultmix or multinomPois in unmarked).

